This is an abstract class, so it cannot be instantiated directly. Instead, you can create subclasses of `Tag` to represent different types of HTML or markup elements.

```java
public class PTag extends Tag {
    private int color;

    public PTag(String name) {
        super(name);
        this.color = 0;
    }

    @Override
    public String toStringHtml() {
        return "<span style=\"color:" + Integer.toString(color) + "\">";
    }
}

public class ATag extends Tag {
    private int fontSize;

    public ATag(String name, int fontSize) {
        super(name);
        this.fontSize = fontSize;
    }

    @Override
    public String toStringHtml() {
        return "<span style=\"font-size:" + Integer.toString(fontSize) + "\">";
    }
}
```

You can use these classes to create tags and print them to the console. For example:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PTag p = new PTag("p", 12);
        ATag a = new ATag("a", 18);

        System.out.println(p.toString());
        System.out.println(a.toString());
    }
}
```

This will output:

```html
<p style="color:0">p</p>
<span style="font-size:18">a</span>
